WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:17.525
- [Interviewer] All right.

00:00:17.525 --> 00:00:18.700
Richard, Richard, where
are you from originally?

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.170
Where'd you grow up?

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:21.840
- I grew up in New York City.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.693
Er Manhattan.

00:00:23.693 --> 00:00:25.080
- [Interviewer] And
how was your childhood?

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.160
You grew up with both your parents?

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.160
- Um, I grew up with my mother.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.311
Both my parents were in the service.

00:00:34.311 --> 00:00:39.311
I was in military school
and, uh, that's it.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:40.763
We took it from there.

00:00:40.763 --> 00:00:43.223
They separated when I was three.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.610
It was a little disturbing, but yeah.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.381
Yeah.

00:00:49.381 --> 00:00:53.056
These are things that you
have to deal with sometimes.

00:00:53.056 --> 00:00:54.570
- [Interviewer] Do you
have brothers and sisters?

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.570
- I have one sister.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.200
- [Interviewer] And
how was your childhood?

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.805
How was your childhood?

00:01:00.805 --> 00:01:02.810
- Kinda crazy.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.040
- [Interviewer] In what ways?

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:09.040
- Um. Physically. Sexually.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:14.730
Uh, It was a lot of different changes.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.840
Um, I mean, if you want
to get somewhere in life

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.560
sometimes you had to do, I mean,

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.560
I was a, uh, I was an escort some time.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:34.473
- [Interviewer] At what age?

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.930
- At 13.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:37.890
- [Interviewer] 13?

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:38.723
- Yeah.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:40.653
- [Interviewer] On whose watch?

00:01:40.653 --> 00:01:41.486
Your mom?

00:01:41.486 --> 00:01:43.630
- No, no.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:47.520
My mom had to work just to keep us afloat

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.987
and uh, I tried not be a burden to her.

00:01:53.997 --> 00:01:57.210
Uh, what I did was I tried
to do the best in school

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.043
that I could.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.060
- [Interviewer] But then
who, at 13 who's kind of?

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.830
- School was maybe five blocks away.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.530
I walked there and I was an alter boy

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:13.110
serving, uh, Cathedral,
St. Patrick's Cathedral

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.913
with Cardinal Cooke O'Connor and uh,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.130
it was a different experience for me.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:23.970
I was an alter boy.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.163
I used to go up to serve
to get me outta trouble.

00:02:30.119 --> 00:02:32.093
I felt at ease.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.629
All I wanted to do is
make something out of my

00:02:37.629 --> 00:02:39.820
my life, just like my dad.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.860
My dad was an electrician.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:46.860
Uh, he was in the Navy and,
uh, I took it from there.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.340
You know, he put, my
mother pushed me as much

00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:00.340
as she could and I did the
most I could to help out.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:03.680
- [Interviewer] So you brought home money?

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.230
- I did.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.883
- [Interviewer] And how
long did this go on?

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.871
- For the rest of my life.

00:03:09.871 --> 00:03:10.704
- [Interviewer] Oh, really?

00:03:10.704 --> 00:03:11.810
- Yeah.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.120
Until mom passed away, so I had moved out.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.720
Got married.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.393
Uh, had my daughter.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.920
My daughter is 21.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.320
She's in college, straight A student.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.153
- [Interviewer] Great.

00:03:26.153 --> 00:03:27.160
- That's so cool.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.670
Uh, I was with San Hard
for most of my life.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.330
Yeah, but they're doing out
here right now is they're

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.890
doing tunnel work out here.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.323
Um, I'm a demolitions expert.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.760
So that's what I did.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.130
It was, it, it was a different experience.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:46.963
I'm red.

00:03:46.963 --> 00:03:48.150
Somebody took me under their wing

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.053
and uh, I learned quite a bit.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.483
It was a real, real experience.

00:03:57.063 --> 00:03:59.432
I mean, on the job training.

00:03:59.432 --> 00:04:02.870
Well, you can't get that anywhere.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.243
- [Interviewer] You get to blow stuff up.

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:06.870
- That's, that's well,
it's not so much just that

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.410
as being safe.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.270
It's, you know, doing the late fuses

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:12.103
and stuff like that.

00:04:12.103 --> 00:04:17.103
Really doing explosions
instead of uh, uh, you know

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:20.470
leaving the pile right there.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.160
Chopping an excavator
down, fill a bucket up and

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.320
send it back up to the top.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.530
Uh, it was, it was cool.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:28.363
- [Interviewer] So that was your career

00:04:28.363 --> 00:04:29.562
for a lot of your life?

00:04:29.562 --> 00:04:31.130
- For quite some time.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.123
Probably 10, 12 years.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:37.070
- [Interviewer] But, but
you say the escorting thing

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.890
was kind of going on for...

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:40.220
- That went on.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:44.777
I was about 20 and uh,
it was, it was weird.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:55.340
I couldn't get used to certain things.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:59.254
I, I did the most I could to make the most

00:04:59.254 --> 00:05:02.004
that I could to help out at home.

00:05:03.347 --> 00:05:04.820
- [Interviewer] Did your parents wonder

00:05:05.688 --> 00:05:06.521
where the money was coming from?

00:05:06.521 --> 00:05:07.354
- Yeah.

00:05:07.354 --> 00:05:08.187
All the time.

00:05:08.187 --> 00:05:09.570
- [Interviewer] And what'd you tell them?

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:10.403
- I lied.

00:05:11.715 --> 00:05:14.350
Uh, there's just certain
things that was just

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:18.340
better left unsaid for me.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:19.580
- [Interviewer] And you weren't
doing it for your own gain.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.670
You were just helping your family.

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:23.240
- I was.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.213
And then I started, yeah.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:31.640
I took an apartment
with uh, this nice lady.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:32.600
Yeah.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.817
The Sheffield Towers on West 57th.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:41.457
And that's where she
ran her whore business.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.140
And she did quite well.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:51.689
So what I did was I was just
pretty much just an arm piece.

00:05:51.689 --> 00:05:53.939
(Chuckles)

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.367
- Uh. It was. It was different.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:05:59.463
- [Interviewer] So you were an art piece

00:05:59.463 --> 00:06:01.500
for the rich ladies on
the Upper East Side?

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:02.498
- Pardon?

00:06:02.498 --> 00:06:03.331
What's that?

00:06:03.331 --> 00:06:04.164
- [Interviewer] You're the art piece

00:06:04.164 --> 00:06:05.611
for the rich ladies on
the Upper East Side?

00:06:05.611 --> 00:06:06.909
- I was.

00:06:06.909 --> 00:06:07.778
I was.

00:06:07.778 --> 00:06:10.278
And they certainly enjoyed it.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:14.873
Yeah.

00:06:14.873 --> 00:06:15.706
You made them feel important.

00:06:15.706 --> 00:06:18.250
You put them on, you know, at the top

00:06:19.184 --> 00:06:24.184
and treat them with some
decor and a lot of respect

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.312
and they liked it.

00:06:28.312 --> 00:06:30.483
You know, liked to feel important.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:34.867
That was cool.

00:06:34.867 --> 00:06:36.290
As a woman should be treated.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:37.250
- [Interviewer] Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.070
How, how long did this go on?

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:40.407
- As I said, til I was 20.

00:06:40.407 --> 00:06:42.430
- [Interviewer] Til you're 20.

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:45.910
And then, it, have you, have
you done anything similar

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:46.978
to that after your twenties?

00:06:46.978 --> 00:06:48.023
- No.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.620
I been made offers and uh,
there's not much just for it.

00:06:56.813 --> 00:06:58.241
There.

00:06:58.241 --> 00:06:59.810
I didn't even bother.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:00.643
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:07:00.643 --> 00:07:02.470
So today you're living on Skid Row.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:05.470
- No, today I'm living in
a Section Eight apartment.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:06.655
- [Interviewer] Oh really?

00:07:06.655 --> 00:07:07.488
- Uh, yeah.

00:07:07.488 --> 00:07:08.321
- [Interviewer] So you got housing?

00:07:08.321 --> 00:07:09.162
- I just moved.

00:07:09.162 --> 00:07:09.995
Uh, what?

00:07:09.995 --> 00:07:10.828
- [Interviewer] You got housing?

00:07:10.828 --> 00:07:11.661
- I did.

00:07:11.661 --> 00:07:12.494
- [Interviewer] Good for you.

00:07:12.494 --> 00:07:13.394
- It only took two years.

00:07:13.394 --> 00:07:14.520
(chuckles)

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:15.497
Uh. Uh.

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:18.300
- [Interviewer] Are you
using any of the drugs

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.180
on the street?

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:20.693
- I did at one point.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:22.593
I did.

00:07:22.593 --> 00:07:23.426
- [Interviewer] Which ones?

00:07:23.426 --> 00:07:24.297
- Huh?

00:07:24.297 --> 00:07:25.130
- [Interviewer] Which drug?

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:25.963
- Heroin.

00:07:25.963 --> 00:07:26.796
- [Interviewer] Heroin.

00:07:26.796 --> 00:07:27.629
- Yeah.

00:07:27.629 --> 00:07:28.462
- [Interviewer] And you got off?

00:07:28.462 --> 00:07:29.600
- Yeah, I did.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:31.760
- [Interviewer] How'd you get off it?

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:32.760
- I was tired of it.

00:07:34.376 --> 00:07:35.209
I was getting older.

00:07:35.209 --> 00:07:36.193
I'm 57.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:44.250
Uh, and I had a lot more important things

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.240
in my life going on.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:47.370
Like my wife.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.195
My daughter going to college.

00:07:49.195 --> 00:07:51.310
I gotta be there for them.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.730
So, so it was important.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:54.810
- [Interviewer] So you're off drugs now?

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:55.643
- Yeah.

00:07:56.569 --> 00:07:58.510
- [Interviewer] How difficult
was it for you get clean?

00:07:58.510 --> 00:07:59.343
Off heroin?

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.820
- It was your determination,
your commitment.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.040
That's all.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.363
You have to want to.

00:08:07.349 --> 00:08:08.932
You gotta be tired.

00:08:15.922 --> 00:08:17.172
Gotta be tired.

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:20.700
- [Interviewer] Looking back at your life,

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.550
What, what do you wish
had happened differently?

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:26.240
Or what, what, what regrets do you have?

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.780
- [Richard] I wish my mother
and father stayed together.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:34.220
Which was uh, important

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:39.220
for me because that was where
I actually got my strength

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:42.160
and my power from.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:44.670
I mean, he pushed me to
where I needed to be.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:45.550
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:48.443
- [Richard] And I trusted him.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:55.550
Which is not the easiest
thing in the world to do.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:00.683
You just don't trust everybody.

00:09:01.782 --> 00:09:04.887
And then when they both passed on, uh,

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:10.450
jeez I just started
growing up at 38 years old.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:13.400
My mother kinda sheltered me

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:18.400
from this jungle and I was
totally unprepared for it.

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:22.780
Didn't know what to expect

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:27.323
but I was going to see what
I was going to experience.

00:09:29.706 --> 00:09:33.263
And, but I'm raising a
daughter and being a miner.

00:09:37.312 --> 00:09:39.029
- [Interviewer] And when
did you come to Los Angeles?

00:09:39.029 --> 00:09:42.640
- Uh? I came to Los
Angeles because I wanted to

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:45.453
do some finish work.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:48.740
- [Interviewer] At what age?

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:53.740
- Uh, I was, jeez I was 54.

00:09:54.102 --> 00:09:55.730
- [Interviewer] Oh, just recently.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:56.563
- Yeah.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:01.153
- [Interviewer] And Skid Row is perhaps

00:10:01.153 --> 00:10:02.900
the worst part of Los Angeles.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:04.863
- It sure was, but I'll tell you what.

00:10:06.987 --> 00:10:10.360
It opened my eyes to a lot of things,

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:11.440
gave me some decision

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.323
and gave me some direction
on where I wanted to go.

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:18.780
I knew I hit one of them like that.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:23.780
I knew my mother put a lot
of effort into teaching me

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:32.127
or showing me how it was and
what was important in her life.

00:10:32.127 --> 00:10:33.754
To her.

00:10:33.754 --> 00:10:37.470
And uh, she always asked me for my advice

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:41.270
and how I felt about
what she was asking me.

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:42.933
I tell her straight out.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:49.050
Uh, it's very important
to have a mentor guide.

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:55.390
If you don't, I tell you when I was,

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:57.270
I did a couple of years in prison.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:01.560
And you see these young
kids, they're locked

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.440
up with their fathers.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:04.763
How do you do that?

00:11:05.825 --> 00:11:08.720
How, how do you do that?

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:12.720
I saw, uh in New Jersey,
what I did is I started a

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.313
transition boarding house.

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:17.690
A halfway house, so to speak.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:18.523
From ISP.

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:22.200
And they had to come
into the house, pay rent,

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:25.003
go to work, make their
meetings, so forth and so on.

00:11:25.003 --> 00:11:28.080
And I was there to help
them and point them

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:29.253
in the right direction.

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:31.513
And it was successful.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:35.463
Being out here.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:40.063
It needs a lot more attention.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:43.993
And it would be, it would
take up a lot of your time.

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:48.440
But I tell you, when it's
all said and done, you'll get

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:52.750
at least a few good guys that really want

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:55.660
to change their lives
and do something with it.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:58.690
You know, it's all I heard out of them was

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.639
I want to do something
different with my life.

00:12:00.639 --> 00:12:02.540
I don't want to work in a goddamn bank.

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:03.627
I don't want to sell drugs.

00:12:03.627 --> 00:12:04.460
I don't want to do this.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.300
I don't want to do that.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:07.900
You know, I want to do something different

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:12.089
that's going to mean
something with my life.

00:12:12.089 --> 00:12:13.690
That was cool.

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:14.763
That inspired me.

00:12:17.782 --> 00:12:19.513
Uh, so they needed a little push.

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:25.014
And not something you can
just read out of a book.

00:12:25.014 --> 00:12:26.190
You know what I mean?

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:30.783
Yes. Or rather, um.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:37.610
Yeah. Yeah, because they weren't
accepted from anybody else.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:46.090
But I don't know if it's not real or

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:49.740
you're not telling them
the truth, you know.

00:12:51.072 --> 00:12:56.072
How to be accepting and
they want to change and do

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:58.410
something different.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:02.090
Me, myself, I certainly wanted
to do something different.

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:05.350
So I was trying to fit in.

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:08.780
You know, I, I wanted to be useful.

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:09.810
I didn't want to be a waste.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:14.810
I know God didn't put me on
this Earth to be a waste.

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:17.388
You know, I mean, you walk around

00:13:17.388 --> 00:13:20.930
and you see some of the people
around here it's like, Jesus

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:22.920
what are you doing every day?

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:23.983
Is this it?

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:29.960
And uh, you have to create your own ideas

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:34.960
and what you want to put out or put forth.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:37.363
That's okay.

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:43.510
Yeah. So like I said,
being a mentor for some

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:49.440
some people are younger gentlemen.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.663
Uh, it's definitely.

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:54.313
I'm not gonna.

00:13:54.313 --> 00:13:56.283
It's the positive way to go.

00:13:57.706 --> 00:13:59.060
You're there.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:01.130
You're showing them the way to go.

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:05.763
You're living and doing as you should do.

